---
import CollectionHeader from "../../components/CollectionHeader.astro";
import { LOCALES, localizePath, nonDefaultLocales, type Locale } from "../../config/i18n";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return nonDefaultLocales().map((locale) => ({
    params: { locale: LOCALES[locale].path },
    props: { locale },
  }));
}

interface Props { locale: Locale }
const { locale } = Astro.props;
const localeInfo = LOCALES[locale];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={localeInfo.ui.aboutTitle}
  description={localeInfo.ui.aboutDescription}
  canonicalPath={localizePath(locale, "/about/")}
  locale={locale}
>
  <div class="shell page narrow collection-page">
    <CollectionHeader
      title="About ThinkerQAQ"
      description="A personal technical site for turning years of learning notes, engineering practice, and ongoing exploration into material that can be revisited and shared."
    />

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How the site is organized</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Articles</strong> are the polished, published pieces that I maintain over time.</li>
        <li><strong>Projects</strong> record things I am exploring, building, or maintaining, together with the results that remain.</li>
        <li><strong>Series</strong> connect related articles into a continuous reading path instead of leaving them as isolated posts.</li>
        <li><strong>Notes</strong> preserve learning records, references, experiments, and ideas that do not need to become formal articles yet.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These sections are not mutually exclusive. An article or note can belong to a series and may also grow out of a project. Projects answer “what did I build?”, series answer “how should this topic be read?”, and notes preserve more of the path that led there.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the notes remain</h2>
      <p>
        Many of the notes predate the current blog by years. Keeping them makes the site more than a collection of newly written summaries: they preserve the original learning process and provide source material for articles that are later rewritten and maintained independently.
      </p>

      <h2>Languages</h2>
      <p>
        Chinese is the primary language of the site. Selected formal articles, series, and learning notes are translated into English. When a real English translation exists, it is published as a first-class localized page; notes that have not been translated yet remain available through the English interface with an explicit Chinese-content notice.
      </p>

      <h2>How it evolves</h2>
      <p>
        The site changes with continued learning and engineering practice. Notes may become articles, related material may be added to a series, and project pages keep the trail from an initial idea to a working result.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
